1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,186
Chapter seven. Twenty-four per second.

2
00:00:03,536 --> 00:00:18,183
In the 1990s, the computer stepped in. In 1993, the dinosaurs of Jurassic Park looked real. And in 1995, Toy Story became the first feature-length film made entirely on a computer.

3
00:00:18,383 --> 00:00:23,741
Then even the film strip disappeared: today, almost everything is shot and projected digitally.

4
00:00:23,941 --> 00:00:29,165
And the highest-grossing movies in history now make nearly three billion dollars.

5
00:00:29,365 --> 00:00:39,323
Yet every movie you watch—in a theater, on TV, or on your phone—is still the exact same thing: still images, one after another. Twenty-four per second.

6
00:00:39,523 --> 00:00:50,776
Your brain does the rest, just like with that horse. And cinema is still looking for the same thing as in that Paris basement: for a group of strangers, in the dark, to watch the same light.

7
00:00:51,726 --> 00:00:55,611
Cinema is still a horse that, for a single instant, flies.

8
00:00:56,561 --> 00:01:01,830
And now we're reading your comments: what story would you like to see in the next episode?
